[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Bag-of-Words (BoW) and TF-IDF in NLP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Natural Language Processing (NLP), computers cannot process raw text directly. Text data must be converted into numerical vectors before feeding it into machine learning models. Two of the foundational techniques for text representation are Bag-of-Words (BoW) and Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Bag-of-Words (BoW) Model"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Bag-of-Words model is one of the simplest methods for feature extraction. It describes the presence of words within a text document. It gets its name because any information about the order or structure of words in the document is ignored; it is just a "bag" of words."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 1: Collect a corpus of documents."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 2: Construct a vocabulary consisting of all unique words across the entire corpus."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Step 3: Create a vector for each document by counting the occurrences of each word from the vocabulary."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing BoW using Python and Scikit-Learn"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer

corpus = [
    "Natural Language Processing is amazing",
    "Machine Learning and Natural Language Processing"
];

vectorizer = CountVectorizer();
X = vectorizer.fit_transform(corpus);

print(vectorizer.get_feature_names_out());
print(X.toarray());"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Limitations of Bag-of-Words"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"While BoW is simple and intuitive, it has major limitations. Common words like "is", "and", or "the" appear very frequently and dominate the word counts, overshadowing more meaningful keywords. Furthermore, BoW does not capture semantic meaning or context."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to TF-IDF"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency (TF-IDF) solves the problem of high-frequency words dominating the representation. It evaluates how important a word is to a document in a corpus."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Term Frequency (TF): Measures how frequently a term occurs in a document. TF = (Number of times term t appears in a document) / (Total number of terms in the document)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inverse Document Frequency (IDF): Measures how important a term is across the entire corpus. IDF = log(Total number of documents / Number of documents containing term t)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"TF-IDF Score: TF * IDF. High scores are reached when the term occurs frequently in a given document and rarely across the rest of the corpus."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing TF-IDF using Python and Scikit-Learn"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer

corpus = [
    "Natural Language Processing is amazing",
    "Machine Learning and Natural Language Processing"
];

tfidf_vectorizer = TfidfVectorizer();
X_tfidf = tfidf_vectorizer.fit_transform(corpus);

print(tfidf_vectorizer.get_feature_names_out());
print(X_tfidf.toarray());"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Bag-of-Words and TF-IDF are foundational text vectorization techniques in NLP. Although modern transformer models (like BERT and GPT) utilize contextual embeddings, BoW and TF-IDF remain crucial for baseline models, information retrieval, and fast keyword extraction tasks."}]
